# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Our user documentation is a core part of the service we provide. It helps our users successfully use services with less friction, less guesswork, and less reliance on our team for support.
This section outlines how we think about documentation, the standards we aim for, and the role docs play in making our service reliable, scalable for increased adoption, and easier to operate - for users and for us.

## Style and standards

Our approach to style and standards for technical documentation is based on GOV.UK recommendations, which emphasise clarity, simplicity, and writing focused on user needs.

Style and standards serve different but complementary purposes.

- Style - how content is written and presented (tone, language, formatting, and structure) to ensure clarity and consistency for readers.
- Standards - defines what must be done and how work is governed (including required processes, quality criteria, and responsibilities) to ensure documentation is reliable, maintainable, and fit for purpose.

### Style

High quality documentation uses consistent style conventions across terminology, structure, formatting and tone.
Consistent conventions make the content easier to read, quicker to understand, and simpler to maintain over time.
By following shared standards, we can make sure our documentation is accurate, accessible, and effective for our users.

#### Follow style guides

Use the GOV.UK style guides when you're writing documentation. We also have repository linting tests we can customise to catch common style errors.
And there's an AI ['Review your technical documentation' review tool](https://m365.cloud.microsoft/chat/?titleId=T_1ed22beb-80ac-c477-a27d-7f9601a54f46&source=embedded-builder) to help you too.

If you have a style question which is not covered by the GOV.UK style guides, speak to a technical writer or content designer.

### Standards

#### User-centred workflow

We follow agreed workflows and processes for how we create and iterate technical documentation which are based on user needs. These are described in [Workflows](/ways-of-working/workflows).

#### Government recommendations

We follow relevant government recommendations for documentation, which include:

- using a docs-as-code approach
- using GOV.UK style guides
- using the GOV.UK Technical Documentation Template
- following the Technology Code of Practice
- following the GOV.UK Service Standard

#### Content reviews before publishing

We use a review process before publishing, separating responsibility for checking technical accuracy from responsibility for writing quality and content choices.
Subject matter experts review documentation to confirm correctness and completeness, while editors or content specialists review for clarity, consistency with style guidance, and suitability for users. This separation helps ensure documentation is both technically sound and easy to understand.

#### Accessible to WCAG 2.2 standards
We meet accessibility standards for public sector websites, including WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines) 2.2, and maintain an up-to-date accessibility statement covering our technical documentation.

## Workflow

Basic principles for documentation work:

- Planned, tracked and signed off using the same tools as other development work
- Part of the 'definition of done' or acceptance criteria for the wider piece of work it's related to
- Tested and iterated with real user input, including user research sessions

### Review process

Every documentation change should be reviewed for technical accuracy by a relevant subject matter expert. Where possible, a technical writer or content design should review the content for style and quality.

Here's a checklist if you're doing a technical review of documentation:

- Are all steps correct and complete?
- Are APIs, commands, or parameters accurately described?
- Are technical concepts explained accurately?
- Are the names of technical items correct?
- If there's sample code, is it helpful and are placeholders clear?

Here's a checklist if you're doing a content review of documentation:

- Is the heading clear and front-loaded with a verb for user tasks?
- Does the content meet the user need?
- Is the content structure coherent and user-centred?
- Is the content grammatically correct?
- Does the content follow our style conventions?
- Is the content accessible, with appropriate link text?
- If there are images, are they necessary - and in SVG format with a text description if so?

